            "english": "Imagine you open your bank app on your phone and send money. Your phone runs Android, the bank server runs on Linux, and the same account is shown on a Windows computer in the bank office. Three different machines, three different systems — but the work is the same. This is where Java is used. The code of the bank is written one time in Java, and then the same code runs on every machine, because every machine has a JVM. A second example is railway ticket booking. Lakhs of people book tickets at the same time. The system must be fast, it must be safe, and it must never stop. Java is chosen for this work because it is fast, it takes care of memory, and many people can work on it at the same time. A third example is in your pocket: most apps on an Android phone are made in Java or in a language like Java. When you open WhatsApp or a game, Java code is running behind it. So learning Java is not only learning one language — it is a way to understand the systems of banks, railways, phones, and big companies.",
